A||||7||2birds||Dragging caliper?!?||||8||
Z||000000||2birds||01-10-2000||01:26 AM||||I have a problem with my right front caliper (I think).  Whenever I put on some left steering, even real slight, the caliper sounds like it is dragging against the rotor.  It has done this ever since I lowered it (with springs).  I've turned the rotors, checked runout, replaced wheel bearings, and replaced the calipers/pads and slider software.  It only does it when I'm driving (can't be repeated on jackstands).  I've checked the rim and rotor shield for wear spots, with no luck.  Any ideas? TIA||||reg||8
Z||000001||Wolfhound||01-10-2000||06:16 AM||||Are you saying, if you brake in a straight line no noise and if you turn without breaking you get the noise?<BR> Have you tried lightly touching the brake while the sound is present to see if the sound changes?<BR> Check for wear on the wheel well liner? Maybe using a mirror to check behind the wheel with the wheel turned to the left and the car on the ground will show you the problem.<BR>Another Thought<BR> If this isn't speed dependent. Try watching the wheel as someone else eases forward in a parking lot.<BR>Good luck! <BR>   <p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 01-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||01-10-2000||12:48 PM||||It's definitely metallic, so I ruled out the inner fender panel rub.  I only get that at full lock.<BR>It happens no matter how fast I'm going, and hitting the brakes doesn't seem to have any effect on the noise.  It doesn't pull to either side when I nail the brakes hard.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WGraffiKs||01-11-2000||07:13 PM||||I'd remove the wheel and the caliper, and look for any obvious misaligned/broken parts.  My vote would be a broken clip on the back of one of the pads, or somehow the pad or caliper is not aligned properly.  If all looks ok, notice the pad wear, look for unevenness that might lead you to the culprit. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-11-2000||07:55 PM||||has the rotor been off recently? sounds like either the bearings aren't right, or the break line is pulling on the calipers. Remember that when you lower with springs you change the hardware angles. check the back of the rim for contact with something. Check the stone sheilds too. you may have bent one too close to the rotor while you where messing with it. check the rears too. it hard to tell from inside if its front or rear. pull the wheels off and look at the sheilds. if they've been hitting you'll see scrub marks on them.<P>BTW.... there is suposed to be a certain amount of side play in the rear hub. even a new one. if you bent a stone sheild even a little it can hit the rotor. I've done it twice now.... anoying as hell but no real damage gets done.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||01-11-2000||08:06 PM||||What about the dust shield rubbing against the rotor?||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||01-11-2000||11:04 PM||||WGraffiKs<BR>The stone shields aren't dragging (had that problem on the back once), I've replaced the pads, calipers, and software, and turned the rotors to eliminate those as possibilities.  The old pads and calipers had no wear spots, either.   I even made sure the spindle and caliper clearances were deburred.<BR>theogre<BR>The brake line pulling the caliper may be possible.  I have the Goodridge braided lines, and I'm using zip ties to hold the line to the upper A-arm, with enough of a loop to allow the line to slide freely.  I may just clamp them down to see what happens.  It just seems odd that ANY slight left steering input causes this.  ||||reg||3
Z||000007||Phil||01-11-2000||04:32 PM||||You gave two important clues in your post <BR>1 It happened right after you lowered the car<BR>2 It does not do it on jack stands<BR>This indicates that you would have to check everything with the suspension compressed. Try putting  jack stands or blocks under the lower A arms pull the wheel and grab a beer and contemplate||||reg||1
